<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Porta Pia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1561">1561</a>–<a href="/years/1565">1565</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Porta Pia is a gate in the Aurelian Walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-iv">Pope Pius IV</a> and built in the 1560s. On 20 September 1870 Italian troops entered Rome through a breach in the walls beside it, ending the temporal power of the popes, and the Capture of Rome is often called simply 'Porta Pia'. Rome became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> the next year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porta-pia.jpg" alt="Porta Pia" width="320" height="295" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Monticiano · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porta_Pia_-_internal_side.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Porta Pia was one of the northern gates in the Aurelian Walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-iv">Pope Pius IV</a>'s civic improvements, named after him. It stands at the end of a new street, the Via Pia, and replaced the Porta Nomentana, a few hundred metres to the south, which was closed up at the same time. The new urban area could no longer reach the Via Nomentana through the ancient gate. According to Vasari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> gave the pope three designs, beautiful but too extravagant, and the pope chose the cheapest. Construction began in 1561 and ended in 1565, after Michelangelo's death, and Giacomo Del Duca carried out the work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>It was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>'s last architectural work. No trace survives of his three drawings, and it is not certain that the gate was built to the original plan. A bronze medal of 1561 by Gian Federico Bonzagna and an engraving of 1568 show it quite differently from its present form. Most experts consider that Michelangelo meant to give gates a picturesque and dramatic front, suited to their symbolic role as an entrance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The gate was set slightly behind the line of the walls and closed the long view along the Via Pia and what is now the Via XX Settembre. A second arch was opened around 1575 to ease traffic, which had grown after the nearby Porta Nomentana was closed.</p><p>The outer façade was completed in 1869 to neoclassical plans by Virginio Vespignani, who seems to have followed an engraving of 1568 closely. It has niches with statues of Saints Agnes and Alexander, by the will of Pius IX, who wished a memorial to his escape from danger when the audience chamber of the convent of Sant'Agnese collapsed during his visit on 12 April 1855.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>On 20 September 1870 Bersaglieri soldiers entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> through a breach opened by artillery in the wall about a dozen metres west of the gate, and completed the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. A marble and bronze monument stands at the breach. In front of the gate is a Monument to the Bersagliere, commissioned by Mussolini and erected in 1932 by Publio Morbiducci. The buildings between the two arches, once the customs office, house the Historical Museum of the Bersaglieri. On 11 September 1926 the antifascist activist Gino Lucetti threw a bomb at the car carrying Benito Mussolini here, without effect.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Porta Pia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Italian troops entered Rome in 1870 through a breach in the walls at <b>Porta Pia</b>, and the popes then lived as self-declared prisoners in the Vatican until the Lateran Treaty of 1929.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>On 20 September 1870 Italian troops entered Rome through the breach at <b>Porta Pia</b>, and Rome became the capital of Italy in 1871.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 20 September 1870 the Bersaglieri entered Rome through a breach at <b>Porta Pia</b>, where 49 Italian soldiers and four officers and nineteen papal troops died.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
